The AND operator
To use the AND bitwise operator, we need the cv2.bitwise_and() function of the OpenCV library. This function requires two parameters—rectangle and circle. These represent the images of a rectangle and circle, respectively.

and_bitwise = cv2.bitwise_and(rectangle, circle)
cv2.imshow('Bitwise AND', and_bitwise)
Note: We must import the cv2 library to use the function.

The AND operator returns the intersecting regions of the two images.



The output of the AND operator
The OR operator
To use the OR bitwise operator, we need the cv2.bitwise_or() function of the OpenCV library. This function requires two parameters—rectangle and circle. These represent the images of a rectangle and circle, respectively.

or_bitwise = cv2.bitwise_or(rectangle, circle)
cv2.imshow('Bitwise OR', or_bitwise)
The OR operator returns the intersecting regions as well as the non-intersecting regions of both images.



The output of the OR operator
The XOR operator
To use the XOR bitwise operator, we need the cv2.bitwise_xor() function of the OpenCV library. This function requires two parameters—rectangle and circle. These represent the images of a rectangle and circle, respectively.

xor_bitwise = cv2.bitwise_xor(rectangle, circle)
cv2.imshow('Bitwise XOR', xor_bitwise)
The XOR operator returns the non-intersecting regions of the two images.



The output of XOR operator
The NOT operator
To use the NOT bitwise operator, we need the cv2.bitwise_not() function of the OpenCV library. This function requires one parameter, circle, which represents the image of a circle.

not_bitwise = cv2.bitwise_not(circle)
cv2.imshow('Circle NOT', not_bitwise)
The NOT operator returns the regions that are not part of white pixels of the image. In our case, it inverts the black and white pixel regions. It shows white pixels on the region outside the circle and black pixels on the circle area.



The output of the NOT operator
